{
  "dom_id": "step-q337118",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14184069/assets/693b38a5c1e5e07fc2791b3d7a471eae47382326a771c1e6ebc49d3cbd167c16.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=60\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=60I=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.003}=\\frac{1000}{3}q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n18=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/9},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{50}{9}\\alpha =\\frac{3}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{27}{500}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{27}{500}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/9}+\\frac{27}{500}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{20},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{20} \\\\ A{e}^{-50(0)/9}+\\frac{27}{500} & =\\frac{1}{20} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{250}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{250}{e}^{-50t/9}+\\frac{27}{500} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}(27-2{e}^{-50t/9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{1}{250}{e}^{-50t/9}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{27}{500}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.",
